<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch10-page2-dynamics-two-strategies" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Two Pure Strategies, and a Look Beyond</title>

  <p>
    Now we generalize what we did for Microsoft vs. Apple to any symmetric
    game with two pure strategies. The payoff matrix has
    the form:
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-ch10-general-2x2">
    <title>A symmetric game with two pure strategies</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Player 2: <m>s_1</m></cell>
        <cell>Player 2: <m>s_2</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Player 1: <m>s_1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(a, a)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(b, c)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Player 1: <m>s_2</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(c, b)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(d, d)</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Reading 1: Section 10.3                                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-10-3">
    <title>Section 10.3, Evolutionary Dynamics with Two Pure Strategies</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 10.3.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Read through the setup, but you can skim the algebra: the
      computations follow the same steps as Microsoft vs. Apple, just with
      letters instead of numbers. Take note of the result at the end and of
      Figure 10.3, which we summarize below!
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Note:</alert> Figure 10.3 has a typo. It labels two of the
      pictures <q>(3)</q>. The bottom-right picture, with <m>a \lt c</m> and
      <m>d \lt b</m>, is case (4).
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Alright, now for a quick summary! After substituting
    <m>p_2 = 1 - p_1</m>, the replicator system becomes the single equation
    <me>
      \dot{p}_1 = p_1(1 - p_1)\bigl((a - c) + (d - b)p_1 - (d - b)\bigr).
    </me>
    Its equilibria are:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>p_1 = 0</m>,
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>p_1 = 1</m>, and
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle p_1^* = \frac{d - b}{(a - c) + (d - b)}</m>,
          provided <m>p_1^*</m> is between 0 and 1.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    For <m>0 \lt p_1 \lt 1</m>, the factors <m>p_1</m> and <m>1 - p_1</m>
    are positive, so the sign of <m>\dot{p}_1</m> comes from the last
    factor. That factor is linear in <m>p_1</m>, so it changes sign at most
    once. This gives the four possible cases shown in Figure 10.3.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ch10-compare-thm-8-5">
    <title>Comparing with Theorem 8.5</title>
    <p>
      These are exactly the four cases of Theorem 8.5 from Chapter 8 (see
      <xref ref="read-8-4-part1" text="title"/>), and two things match up.
    </p>
    <p>
      First, the formula for the interior equilibrium <m>p_1^*</m> is the same as formula (8.5) in the
      book. So the interior equilibrium of the replicator system is exactly
      the mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium
      <m>\sigma^* = p_1^* s_1 + (1 - p_1^*)s_2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Second, here are the four cases, with the stable equilibria of the
      replicator system next to the evolutionarily stable states from
      Theorem 8.5:
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <table xml:id="table-ch10-stable-vs-ess">
    <title>Stable equilibria and evolutionarily stable states</title>
    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>Case</cell>
        <cell>Conditions</cell>
        <cell>Stable equilibria of the replicator equation <m>\dot{p}_1</m></cell>
        <cell>Evolutionarily stable states (Theorem 8.5)</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>(1)</cell>
        <cell><m>a \gt c</m>, <m>d \lt b</m></cell>
        <cell><m>p_1 = 1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s_1</m> (<m>p_1 = 1</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>(2)</cell>
        <cell><m>a \lt c</m>, <m>d \gt b</m></cell>
        <cell><m>p_1 = 0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s_2</m> (<m>p_1 = 0</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>(3)</cell>
        <cell><m>a \gt c</m>, <m>d \gt b</m></cell>
        <cell><m>p_1 = 0</m> and <m>p_1 = 1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s_2</m> (<m>p_1 = 0</m>) and <m>s_1</m> (<m>p_1 = 1</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>(4)</cell>
        <cell><m>a \lt c</m>, <m>d \lt b</m></cell>
        <cell><m>p_1 = p_1^*</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\sigma^*</m> (<m>p_1 = p_1^*</m>)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    The last two columns match in every row. (These four cases assume
    <m>a \neq c</m> and <m>b \neq d</m>.) As the book puts it at the end
    of Section 10.3: <alert>in all cases, the asymptotically stable
    equilibria correspond to evolutionarily stable population
    states.</alert> So for games with two pure strategies, the static idea
    from Chapter 8 (a state that can't be invaded) and the dynamic idea from
    this chapter (a state the population returns to) give the same answer.
    This is what we saw for Microsoft vs. Apple, which is in case (3).
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Hawks and Doves                                              -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ch10-hawks-doves">
    <title>Hawks and Doves</title>
    <p>
      Recall the game of Hawks and Doves from Section 8.4.3. Animals fight
      over a resource using one of two strategies: Hawk (<m>h</m>), which
      fights until injured or until the opponent retreats, and Dove
      (<m>d</m>), which displays hostility but retreats if the opponent
      won't. The parameters are
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>v</m> = value of what they are fighting over,
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>w</m> = cost of injury,
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>t</m> = cost of protracted display,
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      where <m>v</m>, <m>w</m>, and <m>t</m> are all positive and
      <m>v \lt w</m>. The payoff matrix is
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <table xml:id="table-ch10-hawks-doves">
    <title>Hawks and Doves</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Animal 2: <m>h</m></cell>
        <cell>Animal 2: <m>d</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Animal 1: <m>h</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\left(\frac{v - w}{2}, \frac{v - w}{2}\right)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(v, 0)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Animal 1: <m>d</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(0, v)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\left(\frac{v}{2} - t, \frac{v}{2} - t\right)</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Before reading the book's analysis, let's see how much we can figure out
    from Figure 10.3 alone. Take <m>s_1 = h</m> and <m>s_2 = d</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-hd-which-case">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which case of Figure 10.3 is Hawks and Doves in?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Case (1): <m>a \gt c</m> and <m>d \lt b</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Here <m>a = \frac{v - w}{2}</m> and <m>c = 0</m>.
            Since <m>v \lt w</m>, <m>a</m> is negative, so <m>a \lt c</m>: a
            hawk does worse than a dove against a hawk.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Case (2): <m>a \lt c</m> and <m>d \gt b</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. <m>a \lt c</m> is right, but compare <m>d</m> and
            <m>b</m>: <m>d = \frac{v}{2} - t</m> and <m>b = v</m>. Since
            <m>t \gt 0</m>, <m>d \lt b</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Case (3): <m>a \gt c</m> and <m>d \gt b</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Case (3) would mean a hawk does better than a dove
            against a hawk. But <m>a = \frac{v - w}{2} \lt 0 = c</m>, since
            the cost of injury is larger than the value of the resource.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Case (4): <m>a \lt c</m> and <m>d \lt b</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. <m>a = \frac{v - w}{2} \lt 0 = c</m> because
            <m>v \lt w</m>, and <m>d = \frac{v}{2} - t \lt v = b</m> because
            <m>t \gt 0</m>. Each strategy is the best response to the other.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-hd-conclusion">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Knowing only which case Hawks and Doves is in, what can we conclude
        about the evolution of the population?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            From any starting state with <m>0 \lt p_1 \lt 1</m>, the
            population tends toward the mixed state
            <m>\sigma^* = p_1^* h + (1 - p_1^*)d</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. In case (4), <m>p_1^*</m> is the only stable equilibrium,
            and both pure states are unstable. This agrees with Theorem 8.5,
            which says <m>\sigma^*</m> is evolutionarily stable.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Hawks take over the population.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. In case (4), the all-hawk state <m>p_1 = 1</m> is
            unstable. When almost everyone is a hawk, doves do better,
            because they avoid costly injuries.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Doves take over the population.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. In case (4), the all-dove state <m>p_1 = 0</m> is
            unstable. When almost everyone is a dove, a hawk takes the
            resource from every dove it meets.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It depends on the starting state: some populations end up all
            hawks and some end up all doves.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. That describes case (3), with a tipping point. In
            case (4), the arrows on both sides point toward <m>p_1^*</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Reading 2: Section 10.4                                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-10-4">
    <title>Section 10.4, Hawks and Doves Revisited</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 10.4.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The book confirms our conclusion by computing directly. Make sure you
      get the gist of each step. The algebra in step 3 can be skimmed.
      <ol marker="1.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Compute the payoffs <m>\pi_1(h, \sigma)</m>,
            <m>\pi_1(d, \sigma)</m>, and <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Write the replicator equation for <m>\dot{p}_1</m>. (The book
            also writes <m>\dot{p}_2</m>, but we don't need it.)
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Substitute <m>p_2 = 1 - p_1</m> and factor out
            <m>1 - p_1</m>, to get
            <m>\dot{p}_1 = \frac{1}{2}(1 - p_1)\bigl(v + 2t - p_1(w + 2t)\bigr)p_1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the equilibria: <m>p_1 = 0</m>, <m>p_1 = 1</m>, and
            <m>p_1^* = \frac{v + 2t}{w + 2t}</m>, which is the mixed Nash
            equilibrium from Section 8.4.3.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use the phase portrait (Figure 10.4) to determine stability:
            <m>p_1^*</m> is stable.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Notice the book's last sentence: we could have gotten this phase
            portrait from case (4) of Figure 10.3, which is exactly what you
            just did.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </assemblage>



  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Reading 3: Section 10.5 (setup only)                         -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    So far, every population has tended towards an equilibrium. To finish,
    let's take a quick look at what can happen with three strategies. We
    won't go into the mathematical techniques used in this section, so this
    is just a quick look to get the main idea!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-10-5">
    <title>Section 10.5, Side-blotched Lizards</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Skim Section 10.5.</alert> This section is here to show you
      something new: with three strategies, a population doesn't have to
      settle down at an equilibrium at all. Read the background on the
      lizards and look at the payoff matrix. You can skip the rest of the
      math. With three strategies, the replicator system has two variables,
      and we won't be doing multivariable analysis in this class. Then look
      at Figure 10.5 and read the conclusion at the end of the section.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Figure 10.5 shows the direction the population moves from each state in
    the triangle of possible population states. The arrows circle around the
    interior equilibrium
    <m>\sigma^* = \frac{1}{3}O + \frac{1}{3}Y + \frac{1}{3}B</m>. The book
    shows that the population cycles forever. It goes from mostly
    orange-throats, to mostly yellow-stripes, to mostly blue-throats, and
    back again! This behavior is observed in real lizard populations.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a new kind of behavior. With two pure strategies, every
    population tends to an equilibrium. With three, a population might never
    settle down at all. Here <m>\sigma^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium but not an
    evolutionarily stable state, and studying the dynamics shows what the
    population actually does.
  </p>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-10-2" iframe="question-form.html?section=10.2" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
